consiSt("gu",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Guam","d":"Guam’s highest court and its only appellate court: there is no intermediate court of appeals. Three full-time justices (a Chief Justice and two Associate Justices), plus Designated Justices appointed to sit when needed. Hears appeals from final judgments of the Superior Court, discretionary interlocutory appeals, original writs (mandamus, prohibition and similar), certified questions from the Superior Court, and declaratory-judgment requests from the Governor or the Legislature. Has original and appellate jurisdiction over admission and discipline of lawyers and makes the court rules. If a panel splits evenly, the decision below stands (7 GCA § 3108(c)(1))."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"Superior Court of Guam","d":"The single, island-wide court of general jurisdiction for civil, criminal, family, juvenile and probate matters: a Presiding Judge and six other judges by statute (7 GCA §§ 3105, 4101). Also runs traffic court and treatment courts (drug, mental health, veterans, reentry). Hears appeals from the Traffic Division (fines over $150, de novo, notice within 5 court days) and from the Small Claims Division (7 GCA § 4102)."},{"l":"LIMITED JURISDICTION","n":"Small Claims Division of the Superior Court","d":"Claims, counterclaims and cross-claims of $10,000 or less; no attorney’s fees may be awarded. A defendant facing a claim over $5,000 who applies in time is entitled to transfer the case to the regular civil docket (7 GCA §§ 4202–4203)."},{"l":"FEDERAL (SEPARATE)","n":"District Court of Guam","d":"A separate federal territorial court with its own rules and admission, covered elsewhere in Consi. Guam’s pro hac vice rule says it does not govern federal courts or agencies unless they adopt it (GRGAPL 8.01(a)(5))."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Citizen Participation in Government Act of 1998 (7 GCA §§ 17101–17109)","d":"Guam has an anti-SLAPP statute. It was enacted as uncodified P.L. 24-297 (Dec. 3, 1998) and codified by the Compiler as Chapter 17 of Title 7. Its core is an immunity: acts in furtherance of the constitutional right to petition, “including seeking relief, influencing action, informing, communicating and otherwise participating in the processes of government,” are immune from liability regardless of intent or purpose, unless they are not aimed at procuring any government or electoral action, result or outcome (§ 17104). “Government” includes officials and agencies of the United States, any state or territory, and the electorate (§ 17103(a)).\nScope is petitioning, not all speech on public issues: unlike California-style statutes, there is no separate category for statements in a public forum on matters of public interest. The Act is to be construed liberally (§ 17108) and does not displace other defenses (§ 17107).","c":"7 GCA §§ 17101–17105, 17107–17108","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: summary-judgment motion, discovery stay, clear-and-convincing burden on the plaintiff","d":"The Act applies to any motion to dispose of a claim on the ground that it is based on, relates to, or responds to the movant’s protected petitioning (§ 17105). “Motion” is broad: a motion to dismiss, for summary judgment, for judgment on the pleadings, to strike, or any other filing to dispose of a claim (§ 17103(d)). Once filed:\n• the motion is treated as one for summary judgment, heard on the time frame used for preferred or expedited motions;\n• all discovery is suspended until the motion and any appeal are decided;\n• the responding party (the claimant) bears the burden of proof, of going forward, and of persuasion;\n• the court decides on the pleadings and affidavits; and\n• the court must grant the motion and dismiss unless the claimant produces clear and convincing evidence that the movant’s acts are not immune under § 17104.\nThe government body the acts were aimed at, or the Attorney General, may intervene to support the movant.\nNo filing deadline: the statute sets no time limit for bringing the motion, and the GRCP has no rule tied to it.","c":"7 GCA §§ 17103(d), 17105, 17106(a)–(f)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees and sanctions, plus a damages claim (SLAPP-back)","d":"A moving party who wins dismissal must be awarded, “without regards to any limit under Guam law,” the costs of litigation including reasonable attorney and expert witness fees incurred on the motion. The court must also impose sanctions on the responding party, its attorneys or law firms sufficient to deter repetition (§ 17106(g)).\nSeparately, a person harmed by a claim filed in violation of § 17104 may sue the persons responsible for actual or compensatory damages, punitive damages, attorney’s fees and costs (§ 17106(h)).","c":"7 GCA § 17106(g)–(h)","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: expedited appeal of a denial, or of a failure to rule promptly","d":"The moving party has a right to an expedited appeal from an order denying the motion, or from the trial court’s failure to rule on it in expedited fashion (§ 17106(a)(2)). Discovery stays suspended during the appeal (§ 17106(b)). The collected Guam Rules of Appellate Procedure (2014 compilation) contain no rule specific to these appeals, so the notice of appeal timing in GRAP 4(a) (30 days from entry) is the safe assumption; ask for expedited treatment in the Supreme Court.","c":"7 GCA § 17106(a)(2), (b); GRAP 4(a)(1)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: verified application in each court, Guam sponsor of record, $1,250 fee","d":"An out-of-state lawyer (admitted in a U.S. state, territory, commonwealth or D.C., and not disbarred or suspended anywhere) who does not live or regularly work in Guam may be admitted for a particular case. Steps:\n• File a verified application with the court where the case is pending. For a Guam agency proceeding, file with the Supreme Court of Guam.\n• Serve it on all parties who have appeared and on the Office of Regulation Counsel, with proof of service.\n• Send a copy and the fee to the Supreme Court of Guam (3rd floor, Guam Judicial Center) and file proof of payment with the trial court. The Supreme Court’s fee schedule lists the pro hac vice filing fee as $1,250.\n• Name a sponsor: an active Guam bar member who “will be the lawyer of record.” The Guam lawyer stays responsible to the client and for the conduct of the case.\nThe application must disclose (Appendix A): every court where you are admitted; any discipline in the last 5 years; any contempt or sanctions in the last 5 years; and every Guam pro hac vice application in the preceding 2 years.\nAdmission in the Superior Court does not carry over to the Supreme Court on appeal: you must apply again, but no second fee is charged for the same lawyer in the same case.","c":"GRGAPL 8.01(a)–(e) & App. A; Supreme Court of Guam Filing Fee Schedule (Jan. 1, 2014)","v":true},{"t":"Standards, objections and discipline","d":"An application “ordinarily should be granted.” A court may deny or revoke admission if it may harm the prompt and fair administration of justice or other parties’ interests, if the client may be inadequately represented, or if the applicant appears so often that it amounts to regular practice in Guam. There is no numeric cap; frequency is judged case by case.\nThe Office of Regulation Counsel or any party may object with a verified affidavit giving the factual basis, ask for conditions, or move to withdraw an admission already granted. Once you apply, you submit to the Guam courts and the Office of Regulation Counsel for all conduct relating to the case and may be disciplined as a Guam lawyer. You must become familiar with the GRPC, the discipline rules and the local rules.","c":"GRGAPL 8.01(d)(2)–(4), 8.01(f)","v":true},{"t":"Temporary practice without admission (Rule 8.02 and GRPC 5.5)","d":"Without pro hac vice admission, an out-of-state lawyer may: consult with a Guam lawyer about that lawyer’s client; consult with a potential client in Guam about possible retention; prepare for a Guam case while reasonably believing they are eligible for pro hac vice; work in Guam on a case pending or anticipated elsewhere; serve a Guam client from outside Guam; and handle arbitration, mediation or other ADR anywhere. These services are allowed only on a temporary basis and do not permit advertising or holding out as admitted in Guam. GRPC 5.5 is the 2002 ABA multijurisdictional-practice rule.","c":"GRGAPL 8.02(a)–(i); GRPC 5.5(b)–(d)","v":true},{"t":"Full admission: Guam Bar membership required; UBE transfer still needs the local exam portion","d":"No one may practice law in Guam unless a member in good standing of the Guam Bar Association, an integrated bar. Guam administers the NextGen UBE (first given July 2026). A transferred Legacy UBE score of 262+ or NextGen UBE score of 612+, earned within the prior 3 years, is accepted, but the applicant must still pass the local-question portion of the Guam exam and meet character and MPRE requirements. CNMI lawyers admitted by exam may seek reciprocal admission (Rule 9.04). Military spouses stationed in Guam may get a temporary license (Rule 9.03). The bar exam application fee is $1,000.","c":"GRGAPL 7.01(g), 3.09(a), (d), 9.03, 9.04; Supreme Court of Guam Filing Fee Schedule","v":true},{"t":"E-filing access: register only after pro hac vice is granted; 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time","d":"Lawyers admitted in Guam, pro hac vice lawyers and self-represented litigants may register for the Judiciary’s electronic document management system. A pro hac vice lawyer must be admitted first, then register, and gets remote access only to the cases where admitted. The Superior Court eFile page says e-filing covers civil cases and is being expanded to other case types. The rules read as permissive (documents the filer “intends to be filed electronically” go through the system), not as a mandate. A document is timely if filed by 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time on the due date. A system failure never excuses a missed jurisdictional deadline. E-filing does not replace paper service on parties who are not registered e-filers.","c":"EFR 3.1(a), 3.2(a), 3.5(a)(2)(A), 3.11, 5.2(a)(2); Superior Court eFile web page","v":true},{"t":"Limited-scope appearances","d":"A lawyer whose role is limited to one or more matters files a document titled “Limited Appearance.” The lawyer then receives e-service until filing a notice of completion of limited appearance. The client keeps receiving service throughout. GRPC 1.2(c) allows limiting the scope of representation if the limitation is reasonable and the client gives informed consent.","c":"EFR 3.22; GRPC 1.2(c)","v":true},{"t":"Off-island counsel and fax filing in the Superior Court","d":"GRCP 5(f) still governs fax filing. An off-island attorney who has local counsel must fax the document to local counsel, who files a plain-paper copy. An attorney who is not required to have local counsel may fax-file only in emergencies. Faxes received after 5:00 p.m. on the due date are untimely, and the originals must reach the clerk within 14 days.","c":"GRCP 5(f)(1)–(3), (9)","v":true}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Guam Rules of Professional Conduct, based on the 2002 ABA Model Rules","d":"The Supreme Court adopted the GRPC by Promulgation Order No. 04-002 (Sept. 29, 2003), drawing from the 2002 ABA Model Rules. The only amendment since is Rule 1.2(d) (2019). The official text has the Preamble and Scope but no rule-by-rule Comments. As a result, ABA changes made after 2002 are generally not in Guam’s rules. That includes the 2003 confidentiality exceptions, the 2009 lateral screening rule, the 2012 technology and confidentiality amendments, the 2016 Rule 8.4(g), the 2018 advertising rewrite and the 2020 humanitarian exception. The differences below are from the text.","c":"GRPC (PRM 04-002, Sept. 29, 2003; PRM 19-001-01, Nov. 8, 2019)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.6: no crime-fraud or conflict-check exceptions, no stated duty to safeguard data","d":"Guam’s Rule 1.6(b) allows disclosure only to prevent reasonably certain death or substantial bodily harm, to get ethics advice, for lawyer–client controversies and defenses, and to comply with law or a court order. It lacks the ABA exceptions for preventing, mitigating or rectifying client crime or fraud causing substantial financial injury that used the lawyer’s services (ABA 1.6(b)(2)–(3)). It also lacks the exception for detecting conflicts when changing firms (ABA 1.6(b)(7)) and the duty to make reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure (ABA 1.6(c)).","c":"GRPC 1.6(a)–(b)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.2(d): cannabis advice expressly allowed, with a federal-law warning","d":"A lawyer may counsel or assist a client regarding Guam’s marijuana and cannabis-related laws. Where Guam law differs from federal law, the lawyer must also advise the client on federal law and policy.","c":"GRPC 1.2(d) (PRM 19-001-01, Nov. 8, 2019)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.10: no screening cures a lateral lawyer’s conflict","d":"Guam’s Rule 1.10(a) imputes a conflict under Rules 1.7 or 1.9 to every lawyer in the firm. The only exception is a purely personal-interest conflict. There is no ABA-style 1.10(a)(2) exception for screening a laterally hired lawyer with written notice, so a lateral’s former-client conflict disqualifies the firm unless the affected client waives under Rule 1.7.","c":"GRPC 1.10(a), (c)","v":true},{"t":"Rule 1.8(e): no humanitarian gifts to indigent litigation clients","d":"Financial assistance to a client in litigation is limited to advancing court costs and litigation expenses (repayment may be contingent on outcome) and paying those costs for an indigent client. The 2020 ABA exception for modest gifts for food, rent, transportation or medicine to indigent pro bono clients is not in Guam’s rule.","c":"GRPC 1.8(e)","v":true},{"t":"Advertising and misconduct rules are the pre-2018 versions; no Rule 8.4(g)","d":"Guam keeps the older ABA advertising scheme:\n• Rule 7.2: a lawyer may pay only for ads, legal service plans or qualified referral services, and a law practice purchase. Every ad must name at least one responsible lawyer or firm and its office address.\n• Rule 7.3: bans in-person, live telephone and real-time electronic solicitation for pecuniary gain, except of lawyers and family, close personal or prior professional contacts. Written or recorded solicitations of persons known to need services in a particular matter must be labeled “Advertising Material.”\n• Rules 7.4 (specialization) and 7.5 (firm names and letterheads) remain.\n• Rule 8.4 has paragraphs (a)–(f) only: there is no harassment or discrimination provision like ABA 8.4(g).","c":"GRPC 7.2, 7.3, 7.4, 7.5, 8.4","v":true},{"t":"Trust accounts: records for five years","d":"Client and third-party funds go in a separate account maintained where the lawyer’s office is, unless the client or third person consents to another location. Complete records must be kept for five years after the representation ends (the official text still shows “[five years]” in brackets, copied from the ABA model). Advance fees go into the trust account until earned.","c":"GRPC 1.15(a), (c)","v":true}],"style":"Territory of Guam","home":"https://guamcourts.gov/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"Post-judgment motions: 10 days, counted on the calendar since October 2023","d":"Renewed judgment as a matter of law, amended findings, new trial, and motions to alter or amend the judgment are all due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment. Federal practice allows 28 days. The court cannot extend these deadlines. Since PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023), GRCP 6(a) counts every day, including weekends and holidays, so the 10 days are calendar days. A Rule 60 motion tolls the appeal time only if filed within 10 days after judgment.","c":"GRCP 6(a)(1), 6(b)(2), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); GRAP 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)"},{"t":"Statement of jurisdiction within 10 days of the notice of appeal, or the appeal is dismissed","d":"Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must submit a statement of jurisdiction to the Supreme Court. It must give the basis for finality or appealability, the statutory basis for jurisdiction, and the docket-entry date, with a legible copy of the judgment showing its entry date. If it is not filed, or if it shows no final appealable judgment has been entered, the appeal is dismissed for lack of jurisdiction. Within the same 10 days, order the transcript (or certify none) and file the representation statement.","c":"GRAP 4.1(a)–(b), 7(b)(1), 8(b)"},{"t":"Two clocks: the Supreme Court skips weekends for short periods, the Superior Court does not","d":"GRAP 11(a)(1)(B) still excludes Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays from periods shorter than 11 days, unless stated as calendar days. GRCP 6(a), as amended in 2023, counts every day. A 10-day deadline in the Supreme Court can therefore end days later than a 10-day deadline in the Superior Court. GRAP adds 3 calendar days after service, including electronic service. GRCP adds 3 days after service by mail, clerk-leaving or consented means, including e-service.","c":"GRAP 11(a)(1), 11(c); GRCP 6(a), 6(d)"},{"t":"Answer in 20 days; Government of Guam gets 60; serve within 90","d":"A defendant must answer within 20 days after service of the summons and complaint. The Government of Guam and its officers or agencies get 60 days after service on the Attorney General or agency counsel. After a ruling on a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days. A plaintiff must serve within 90 days after filing; this was 180 days before October 19, 2023. A waiver request must allow 30 days (60 if the defendant is outside Guam).","c":"GRCP 12(a), 4(d)(1)(F), 4(m)"},{"t":"Motion briefing runs from the motion’s filing date, and oral argument must be requested","d":"In Superior Court civil cases, an opposition is due 28 days after the motion is filed, not after service. The reply is due 42 days after the motion is filed. Parties may extend each by stipulation by no more than 7 days, and late papers are not considered without leave. Memoranda are limited to 20 pages and replies to 10. Request oral argument on the notice of motion or Form 2 within 7 days after the last paper is due. If you do not, the request is waived and the court may rule on the papers.","c":"CVR 7.1(d), (e)(2)–(3), (f), (g)"},{"t":"One set of interrogatories, 50 questions; discovery motions need a joint stipulation","d":"Without leave, each party may serve only one set of interrogatories and one set of requests for admission on any other party. Each set is capped at 50 including every subpart. Answers must quote each question in full. Before any discovery motion, counsel must meet and confer. If disputes remain, they must file with the motion a written stipulation setting out each issue verbatim with each side’s contentions; otherwise the court will not hear the motion. GRCP 26(b)(1) still uses the pre-2015 scope (“reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence”), with no proportionality language.","c":"CVR 33.1(a)–(b), 37.1(a)–(b); GRCP 26(b)(1), 33(a)"},{"t":"Jury demand within 10 days after the last pleading","d":"A jury must be demanded in writing and served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading directed to the issue. Failure to serve and file the demand waives a jury. GRCP 6(a) now counts those 10 days on the calendar.","c":"GRCP 38(b), (d); GRCP 6(a)"},{"t":"Appeal mechanics: file in the Superior Court and pay the Supreme Court’s fee at the same time","d":"The notice of appeal is filed with the Clerk of the Superior Court within 30 days after entry of the judgment or order. The appellant must also send the Supreme Court a file-stamped copy. The non-refundable Supreme Court docket fee ($375 on the posted schedule) is paid to the Clerk of the Supreme Court when the notice is filed below, and a cross-appellant pays its own fee. A second party may appeal within 14 days after the first notice.","c":"GRAP 3(a), 4(a)(1), (3), 8(a); Supreme Court of Guam Filing Fee Schedule"},{"t":"Pro hac vice does not follow the case up on appeal","d":"Admission in the Superior Court does not carry over to the Supreme Court. File a new verified application there; no second fee is charged for the same lawyer in the same case. Your Guam sponsor is the lawyer of record throughout.","c":"GRGAPL 8.01(e)(2), App. A(9)"},{"t":"Judge challenge must come before the first fact hearing","d":"No peremptory challenge exists. A verified for-cause statement must be filed at the earliest practicable opportunity after you learn of the ground, and in all events before the hearing of any issue of fact starts. Waiting until after an adverse ruling at trial forfeits the challenge.","c":"7 GCA § 6107(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Appellate briefs: 14-point type, colored covers, excerpts of record in every case","d":"Proportional fonts must be 14-point or larger, with serifs in the text. Page and word limits:\n• Opening and answering briefs: 30 pages or 14,000 words.\n• Reply briefs: 15 pages or half the word count.\n• In cross-appeals, the appellee’s principal and response brief may run 35 pages or 16,500 words.\nThe court disfavors motions to exceed the limits; file one at least 14 days before the brief is due (7 days for a reply). Covers are color-coded. Excerpts of record are required in every appeal (3 copies, filed with the opening brief). You may not incorporate trial-court briefs by reference.","c":"GRAP 13.2(e), 15(a)–(b), 16(a)(2), (5), (7)"},{"t":"Summary judgment: 30 days after discovery closes, with a statement of undisputed facts","d":"Unless the court orders otherwise, summary judgment motions may be filed up to 30 days after the close of all discovery. A motion must include a statement of the issues and a separate “Statement of Undisputed Material Facts” with record citations. The opposing party must respond to that statement.","c":"GRCP 56(b); CVR 56.1"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge of a judge; for-cause disqualification only","d":"Guam has no peremptory (no-cause) judge challenge. The 1985 drafters considered and rejected California’s affidavit-of-prejudice procedure (Cal. Code Civ. Proc. § 170.6), fearing the bar would make “de facto assignments of judges.” Grounds track 28 U.S.C. § 455. A judge must step aside where impartiality might reasonably be questioned, and for personal bias, prior involvement as a lawyer or government counsel, financial interest, or close family involvement. Unlike federal law, after full disclosure all parties may agree to let the judge continue on any ground (§ 6105(a)–(b)). The judge must declare a known ground in open court and have it entered in the minutes (§ 6106).","c":"7 GCA §§ 6105, 6106; 7 GCA § 6107, 1985 Comment","v":true},{"t":"Disqualification procedure: verified statement before any fact hearing; 10 days to answer","d":"If the judge does not declare a disqualification, a party who has appeared may file and serve a written, verified statement of the facts. It must be filed at the earliest practicable opportunity after discovering the facts, and in any event before the hearing of any issue of fact begins. Serve it on every party and on the judge.\nThe judge has 10 days to consent in writing or file a verified answer. Failing to answer within 10 days counts as disqualification. A judge who denies the grounds may not decide the question. The Presiding Judge (or the next senior judge, if the Presiding Judge is challenged) assigns another judge within 5 days after the clerk’s notice.\nFor a Justice, the Supreme Court decides without the challenged Justice, and a pro tempore Justice replaces one who is disqualified. The court’s judicial disqualification page posts party forms adopted by Promulgation Order No. 13-003-01 (Nov. 21, 2013).","c":"7 GCA § 6107(a)–(e); PRM 13-003-01","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Petitioning activity has statutory immunity regardless of motive","d":"The Citizen Participation in Government Act makes acts in furtherance of the right to petition (seeking relief, influencing action, informing and communicating with government or the electorate) immune from liability “regardless of intent or purpose.” The only exception is conduct not aimed at procuring any government or electoral action, result or outcome. This is a substantive defense, not only a procedural motion, and the Act says it does not limit other constitutional, statutory or common-law protections.","c":"7 GCA §§ 17104, 17107","v":true},{"t":"Organic Act bill of rights: not reviewed","d":"Guam has no constitution of its own. Its civil-rights guarantees, including speech and petition, are in the federal Organic Act of Guam (48 U.S.C. § 1421b). The Organic Act text and Guam case law were not in the collected sources, so whether Guam courts read these protections more broadly than the First Amendment was not checked.","c":"48 U.S.C. § 1421b (not in collected texts)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Governor and Legislature can ask the Supreme Court for declaratory judgments","d":"The Governor (in writing) or the Legislature (by resolution) may ask the Supreme Court for a declaratory judgment on the interpretation of any law within the Guam courts’ jurisdiction and on questions about their own powers and duties. The Court may act only on matters of great public interest where normal process would cause undue delay. Private parties cannot use this procedure, but interested parties must be allowed to be heard.","c":"7 GCA § 4104","v":true},{"t":"Evenly divided panel affirms; unpublished memorandum opinions cannot be cited","d":"If a Supreme Court panel is evenly divided, the decision appealed from is sustained. Opinions are published unless designated otherwise. Memorandum opinions are never published, and unpublished opinions may not be cited except for law of the case, res judicata, collateral estoppel, or a criminal case involving the same respondent.","c":"7 GCA § 3108(c)(1); GRAP 27(b)(1)","v":true},{"t":"Court boxes: service is complete when the court drops a paper in your box","d":"Every active Guam Bar member practicing in Guam with a Guam office is assigned a box at the Superior Court Clerk’s office. The court serves orders, judgments and notices by depositing them in the box, and service is complete on deposit. Check the box: deadlines can run from that deposit. Only the court may serve this way.","c":"GRCP 5(g); GRAP 10(c)","v":true},{"t":"Bluebook citations; supply copies of hard-to-find authority","d":"Citations must follow the most recent edition of The Bluebook. A party must give the court a copy of any authority it cites that is not available in the Guam Law Library or a generally recognized legal research database (for example, decisions of other Pacific island courts).","c":"GR 4.1","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Guam Rules of Appellate Procedure (GRAP), official Judiciary of Guam compilation “as of February 24, 2014” (adopted PRM 07-003-01, Feb. 21, 2007; amended through PRM 07-003-06, Feb. 24, 2014), https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRAP_20140224.pdf; Guam Rules of Civil Procedure (amended through PRM 06-006-24, eff. Oct. 19, 2023) Rules 6, 23(f), 50, 52, 54, 58, 59, 60, 62; 7 GCA §§ 3107–3108, 25102 (Compiler of Laws, col.guamcourts.gov); 7 GCA § 17106 (Citizen Participation in Government Act); Judiciary of Guam Electronic Filing Rules (PRM 15-001-01, Oct. 2, 2015); Supreme Court of Guam fee schedule (Jan. 1, 2014). The posted GRAP compilation is dated 2014; no later GRAP amendment appears in the promulgation-order index checked, but confirm with the Supreme Court Clerk.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Guam: one appellate court, a 30-day notice, and a 10-day jurisdiction statement","d":"Guam has no intermediate appellate court. Every appeal from the Superior Court goes directly to the three-justice Supreme Court of Guam, and Supreme Court decisions can be reviewed by the U.S. Supreme Court on certiorari (GRAP 27(c)(4)(B), 27(d)). GRAP is modeled on the federal appellate rules (with Ninth Circuit features such as excerpts of record), so the structure will feel familiar. The traps are local:\n• The notice of appeal is filed in the Superior Court, but the Supreme Court docket fee is due at the same time.\n• A statement of jurisdiction must reach the Supreme Court within 10 days, or the appeal is dismissed.\n• Post-judgment motions that toll the appeal time are due within 10 days, not 28.\n• GRAP and GRCP count short periods differently."},"groups":[["What is appealable",[{"t":"Final judgments, plus interlocutory orders made appealable by statute","d":"Appellate review is available only after final judgment in the Superior Court, except where a law provides for immediate review. 7 GCA § 25102 makes these civil orders appealable:\n• orders after an appealable judgment;\n• orders quashing service of summons;\n• orders granting a new trial or denying judgment notwithstanding the verdict;\n• orders discharging or refusing to discharge an attachment;\n• orders granting, dissolving, or refusing an injunction;\n• orders appointing a receiver;\n• certain interlocutory judgments in redemption, partition and divorce cases;\n• probate orders made appealable by Title 15.\nGRCP 54(b) certification makes a partial judgment final.","c":"7 GCA §§ 3107(b), 3108(a), 25102; GRCP 54(b)"},{"t":"Discretionary interlocutory appeal by petition","d":"Other interlocutory orders can be reviewed only at the Supreme Court’s discretion, where resolving the question of law will (1) materially advance the end of the litigation or clarify further proceedings, (2) protect a party from substantial and irreparable injury, or (3) clarify issues of general importance in the administration of justice. File a verified petition for permission to appeal with the Supreme Court Clerk. It is due within the time set by the authorizing statute or rule, or otherwise within the GRAP 4(a) time (30 days after entry). Serve all parties and the Superior Court Clerk. The petition must state which § 3108(b) ground applies, attach the order, and include points and authorities. It is limited to 15 pages, with an original and 1 copy. An answer or cross-petition is due within 7 days after service. If permission is granted, pay the fees within 10 days; the grant order serves as the notice of appeal. The Court may revisit jurisdiction later.","c":"7 GCA § 3108(b); GRAP 4.2(a)–(d)"},{"t":"Class certification and anti-SLAPP orders","d":"The Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying class certification if application is made within 10 days after entry. The appeal does not stay proceedings unless ordered. A party whose anti-SLAPP motion under the Citizen Participation in Government Act is denied, or not ruled on expeditiously, has a statutory right to an expedited appeal, and discovery stays suspended meanwhile. GRAP sets no special procedure for these appeals.","c":"GRCP 23(f); 7 GCA § 17106(a)(2), (b)"},{"t":"Writs of mandamus and prohibition","d":"A petition for a writ directed to the trial court is titled “In re (petitioner)” and must be verified. It states the relief sought, the issues, the facts and the reasons the writ should issue, with the essential order and record attached. Serve all parties and give a copy to the trial judge. Name the Superior Court as respondent, not the judge by name, and list each real party in interest. Writ petitions are limited to 30 pages and must include the certificate of interested parties. The Court may deny without an answer, issue an alternative writ, or order an answer. Writ proceedings take preference over ordinary civil cases, and writ decisions are effective on issuance.","c":"7 GCA § 3107(b); GRAP 24(a)–(g)"}]],["Notice of appeal and tolling",[{"t":"30 days from entry; file in the Superior Court","d":"In a civil case, the notice of appeal must be filed with the Superior Court within 30 days after the judgment or order is entered. Give the Superior Court Clerk enough copies for service, and submit a file-stamped copy to the Supreme Court. A notice filed after the decision is announced but before entry is treated as filed on the date of entry. A notice mistakenly filed in the Supreme Court is forwarded and treated as filed below on the date received. Only a timely notice is jurisdictional: other missed steps lead to whatever action the Court considers appropriate, which may include dismissal. Suggested form: GRAP Form 1.","c":"GRAP 3(a), 3(c), 4(a)(1)–(2), 4(d)"},{"t":"Entry of judgment: separate document, or 150 days","d":"Where a separate document is required, the judgment is “entered” when it is on the docket and either set out on a separate document or 150 days have run from docketing, whichever comes first. Orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 fee, 59 and 60 motions need no separate document.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(7); GRCP 58(a)–(b)"},{"t":"Tolling motions: 10 days after judgment","d":"A timely motion under GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59 (new trial or alter or amend), or 60 restarts the appeal time for all parties from entry of the order disposing of the last such motion. A Rule 60 motion counts only if filed within 10 days after judgment. A fee motion under Rule 54(d)(2) counts only if the court orders it to under Rule 58(c). Each of those GRCP motions must be filed no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, and the court cannot extend that time. Since October 19, 2023 the GRCP counts every calendar day. A notice of appeal filed while such a motion is pending takes effect when the motion is decided. To challenge the ruling on the motion itself, file a new or amended notice (no extra fee).","c":"GRAP 4(a)(4); GRCP 6(a)–(b), 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e), 54(d)(2), 58(c)"},{"t":"Cross-appeals and other parties: 14 days after the first notice","d":"If one party files a timely notice, any other party may file within 14 days after the first notice was filed, or within the regular 30 days, whichever ends later. The second party to appeal is the cross-appellant and must pay its own docket fee when filing its notice. Parties may file a joint notice, and appeals may be consolidated.","c":"GRAP 3(b), 3(e)(2), 4(a)(3), 8(a)"},{"t":"Extending or reopening the time to appeal","d":"The Superior Court may extend the time if a party moves no later than 14 days after the 30-day period expires and shows excusable neglect or good cause. Note: the rule text also refers to motions filed “during the thirty (30) days after” expiry, an internal inconsistency; move within 14 days to be safe. No extension may exceed 30 days after the original deadline or 10 days after the order granting it, whichever is later. A party who did not receive notice of entry within 21 days may move to reopen within 180 days after entry or 7 days after receiving notice, whichever is earlier. If granted, the window is 14 days. Lack of notice from the clerk does not otherwise excuse a late appeal.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(5)–(6); GRCP 77(e)"},{"t":"Statement of jurisdiction within 10 days, or dismissal","d":"Within 10 days after filing the notice of appeal, the appellant must submit a statement of jurisdiction. It sets out why the judgment is final or otherwise appealable, the statutory basis of jurisdiction, and the docket date of the judgment, with a legible copy of the judgment showing the entry date. If no statement is filed, or if it shows that no final appealable judgment is on the docket, the case “shall be dismissed for lack of jurisdiction.” A case dismissed as premature may be re-filed under GRAP 4 once judgment is entered.","c":"GRAP 4.1(a)–(b) & Note"}]],["Fees, record and docketing",[{"t":"Docket fee due when the notice is filed","d":"When filing the notice of appeal in the Superior Court, the appellant must pay the required non-refundable docket fee to the Clerk of the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court’s posted schedule (January 1, 2014) lists $375 for new appeals. The attorney who filed the notice must file a representation statement within 10 days, naming the parties represented and all other parties and counsel.","c":"GRAP 8(a)–(b); Supreme Court of Guam Filing Fee Schedule"},{"t":"Transcript: order within 10 days","d":"Within 10 days after the notice of appeal (or after the last tolling motion is decided), the appellant must either order the needed transcript in writing, filing the order with the Superior Court Clerk and copying the Supreme Court Clerk, or certify that none will be ordered. If you will argue that a finding lacks evidentiary support, you must include all relevant evidence. For a partial transcript, serve a statement of issues within the same 10 days. The appellee may designate more parts within 10 days, then order them or move to compel. The reporter has 45 days to finish, extendable by the Supreme Court Clerk.","c":"GRAP 7(b)(1)–(4), 7.1(b)(1)"},{"t":"Record is “filed” when the certificate of record is filed","d":"The record stays in the Superior Court. When the transcript is filed, or once the clerk learns that none will be ordered, the Superior Court Clerk files a certificate of record with the Supreme Court. The record is then considered filed, and the clerk notifies the parties of the briefing schedule. The opening-brief clock runs from this date.","c":"GRAP 7.1(c)"}]],["Briefing, length and form",[{"t":"Schedule: 40 / 30 / 14 days","d":"Opening brief: 40 days after the record is filed. Answering brief: 30 days after service of the opening brief. Reply: 14 days after service of the answering brief, and, absent good cause, at least 14 days before argument. In cross-appeals, the appellant’s response-and-reply brief is due 30 days after the appellee’s principal and response brief, and the appellee’s reply is due 14 days later (at least 3 days before argument). Dates set by court order get no extra 3 days for service.","c":"GRAP 13.2(f), 17(a), 17(d)(1)"},{"t":"Extensions: one 14-day clerk extension, then written motion","d":"For good cause, the Clerk may grant one oral (even telephone) extension of up to 14 days on prior notice to the other side. After that, only a written motion showing extraordinary and compelling circumstances will work. Any other extension needs a written motion filed at least 5 days before the due date, with an affidavit covering the due date, prior extensions, reasons, the time needed, and assurance of filing. The affidavit is required even if opposing counsel stipulates.","c":"GRAP 17(c)"},{"t":"Length and form","d":"A principal brief may be 30 pages or 14,000 words (1,300 monospaced lines). A reply may be 15 pages or half the words. In cross-appeals, the appellee’s principal and response brief may be 35 pages or 16,500 words. Include a certificate of compliance when relying on word count. The court disfavors motions to exceed the limits; file one at least 14 days before the brief is due (7 for a reply), with a detailed affidavit. Use 14-point or larger serif type, double-spaced, on 8½ × 11 paper with 1-inch margins. Covers are color-coded: appellant blue, appellee red, intervenor or amicus green, reply gray, supplemental tan. You may not incorporate trial-court briefs by reference. File an original and 1 copy.","c":"GRAP 13.2(e), 16(a)(2)–(7), 17(b)"},{"t":"Excerpts of record are mandatory","d":"Every appeal requires excerpts of record: 3 copies, bound separately, filed with the opening brief, and 1 served on each party. They must include at least the notice of appeal, the trial docket sheet, and the judgment or order appealed. Volumes over 300 pages must be split.","c":"GRAP 15(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Motions and amicus briefs","d":"A response to a motion is due 7 days after service. A party that does not oppose must file a statement of non-opposition within 7 days. A reply is due 5 days after the response. Motions and responses are limited to 20 pages, replies to 10. Emergency motions need “Emergency” in the title and an affidavit, and you must notify the Clerk early. The Government of Guam may file amicus briefs without leave; others need consent or leave. Amicus briefs are due 7 days after the supported party’s principal brief and are limited to half the party’s length.","c":"GRAP 6(a)(3)–(4), 6(e), 6(f)(2), 14(a), (d)–(e)"}]],["Argument, decision, rehearing and further review",[{"t":"Oral argument is the default","d":"Oral argument must be allowed unless a three-justice panel unanimously finds the appeal frivolous, the issue authoritatively decided, or argument unnecessary. Each side gets 30 minutes, including questions. Requests for more time go by letter to the Clerk within 7 days of the argument date. A decision needs a majority of the panel, and an evenly divided panel leaves the decision below in place.","c":"GRAP 19(a), (c); 7 GCA § 3108(c)(1); GRAP 27(a)"},{"t":"Rehearing: 14 days (45 if the Government of Guam is a party)","d":"A petition for rehearing is due within 14 days after entry of judgment. In a civil case where the Government of Guam or its officer or agency is a party, any party has 45 days. The petition must state with particularity the points overlooked or misapprehended. An issue not previously briefed cannot be raised for the first time. Limit: 15 pages, orange cover. No answer is allowed unless the Court requests one, and rehearing is ordinarily not granted without one. Petitions for rehearing en banc follow the same deadline. A petition for initial hearing en banc is due by the date the appellee’s brief is due.","c":"GRAP 29(b)–(c), 30(a)–(c)"},{"t":"Mandate, costs and interest","d":"The mandate issues 7 calendar days after the rehearing time expires, or 7 days after denial of a timely rehearing petition or stay motion. A bill of costs is due within 14 days after entry of judgment, and objections within 10 days after service. After a remand, the Supreme Court keeps no jurisdiction, so review of the proceedings on remand needs a new notice of appeal. Frivolous appeals can draw just damages and single or double costs.","c":"GRAP 21, 22(d), 27(c)(2), (5), 28"},{"t":"Review by the U.S. Supreme Court","d":"A party may move to stay the mandate pending a petition for certiorari to the Supreme Court of the United States. The motion must show a substantial question and good cause. The stay is limited to 90 days unless extended or unless the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within that period. File a copy of any certiorari petition with the Supreme Court of Guam. The federal statute authorizing certiorari (in the Organic Act, 48 U.S.C.) was not among the collected texts.","c":"GRAP 27(c)(4)(B), 27(d)"}]],["Computing time, stays and e-filing",[{"t":"GRAP excludes weekends from periods under 11 days","d":"Unlike the 2023 GRCP, GRAP 11 still excludes intermediate Saturdays, Sundays and legal holidays (as defined in 1 GCA § 1000) when a period is shorter than 11 days, unless stated as calendar days. Add 3 calendar days after service unless the paper is delivered on the date of service, and electronic service does not count as delivered that day. The last day for electronic filing ends at midnight; EFR 3.11 sets 11:59 p.m. Chamorro Standard Time. The Court cannot extend the time for a notice of appeal or petition for permission to appeal, except as Rule 4 allows.","c":"GRAP 11(a)–(c); EFR 3.11(a)(2)"},{"t":"Stays and supersedeas","d":"Move first in the Superior Court for a stay, approval of a supersedeas bond, or an injunction pending appeal. A motion in the Supreme Court must show that moving below was impracticable or was denied. Execution is automatically stayed for 10 days after entry of judgment, except injunction and receivership judgments. A supersedeas bond stays the judgment once approved. The Government of Guam needs no bond.","c":"GRAP 12(a); GRCP 62(a), (d), (e)"},{"t":"E-filing in the Supreme Court","d":"Lawyers admitted in Guam, pro hac vice lawyers, and self-represented litigants may register for the Judiciary’s electronic system; one registration covers both courts. The Supreme Court may convert a pending case to e-filing. A system failure never excuses a missed jurisdictional deadline, so file by any available means. Out-of-state counsel must be admitted pro hac vice in the Supreme Court separately from the Superior Court.","c":"EFR 3.11(b), 9.1(b), 9.3(a); GRAP 10(a)(2)(D), 36; GRGAPL 8.01(e)(2)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Notice of appeal, civil (filed in Superior Court)","d":"30 days from entry of the judgment or order appealed from.\nJurisdictional. Pay the Supreme Court docket fee ($375 on posted schedule) at filing (GRAP 8(a)).","c":"GRAP 4(a)(1), 3(a)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the judgment or order appealed from)","note":"Jurisdictional. Pay the Supreme Court docket fee ($375 on posted schedule) at filing (GRAP 8(a))."}},{"t":"Notice of appeal by another party / cross-appeal","d":"14 days from filing of the first notice of appeal (or the regular 30 days, if later).\nCross-appellant pays its own docket fee.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(3)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the first notice of appeal)","note":"Cross-appellant pays its own docket fee."}},{"t":"Statement of jurisdiction","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal.\nMandatory dismissal if not filed. GRAP 11 excludes weekends and holidays from periods under 11 days.","c":"GRAP 4.1(a)–(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":"Mandatory dismissal if not filed. GRAP 11 excludes weekends and holidays from periods under 11 days."}},{"t":"Order transcript or certify none; representation statement","d":"10 days from filing of the notice of appeal (transcript: or decision on last tolling motion, if later).","c":"GRAP 7(b)(1), 8(b)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the notice of appeal)","note":""}},{"t":"Post-judgment motions (GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59 new trial or alter/amend)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nCannot be extended. Calendar days since Oct. 19, 2023 (GRCP 6(a)). Timely motion restarts appeal time (GRAP 4(a)(4)).","c":"GRCP 50(b), 52(b), 59(b), (e); GRCP 6(b)(2)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Cannot be extended. Calendar days since Oct. 19, 2023 (GRCP 6(a)). Timely motion restarts appeal time (GRAP 4(a)(4))."}},{"t":"Rule 60 motion that tolls appeal time","d":"10 days from entry of judgment.\nLater Rule 60 motions (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3) do not toll.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(4)(A)(vi)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Later Rule 60 motions (up to 1 year for reasons 1–3) do not toll."}},{"t":"Attorney’s fee motion","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nTolls appeal time only if the court so orders under GRCP 58(c).","c":"GRCP 54(d)(2)(B); CVR 54.3","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Tolls appeal time only if the court so orders under GRCP 58(c)."}},{"t":"Motion to extend time to appeal","d":"14 days from expiration of the 30-day appeal period.\nExcusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(5)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of the 30-day appeal period)","note":"Excusable neglect or good cause. Extension capped at 30 days past the deadline or 10 days after the order, whichever is later."}},{"t":"Motion to reopen time to appeal","d":"180 days from entry of judgment (or 7 days after receiving notice, if earlier).\nOnly if notice of entry was not received within 21 days. Reopened window is 14 days.","c":"GRAP 4(a)(6)","due":{"n":180,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Only if notice of entry was not received within 21 days. Reopened window is 14 days."}},{"t":"Petition for permission to appeal (interlocutory)","d":"30 days from entry of the order (unless the authorizing statute or rule sets another time).\nAnswer or cross-petition 7 days after service. 15-page limit.","c":"GRAP 4.2(a)(2), 4(a)(1); 7 GCA § 3108(b)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Answer or cross-petition 7 days after service. 15-page limit."}},{"t":"Application to appeal class-certification order","d":"10 days from entry of the order.\nDiscretionary. No automatic stay.","c":"GRCP 23(f)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of the order)","note":"Discretionary. No automatic stay."}},{"t":"Opening brief and excerpts of record","d":"40 days from filing of the record (certificate of record).","c":"GRAP 17(a), 13.2(f)(1), 15(b), 7.1(c)","due":{"n":40,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the record)","note":""}},{"t":"Answering brief","d":"30 days from service of the opening brief.","c":"GRAP 17(a), 13.2(f)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (service of the opening brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Reply brief","d":"14 days from service of the answering brief.\nAlso at least 14 days before argument absent good cause. In a cross-appeal the appellant’s response-and-reply is due in 30 days and the appellee’s reply in 14 (GRAP 13.2(f)(3)–(4)).","c":"GRAP 17(a)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (service of the answering brief)","note":"Also at least 14 days before argument absent good cause. In a cross-appeal the appellant’s response-and-reply is due in 30 days and the appellee’s reply in 14 (GRAP 13.2(f)(3)–(4))."}},{"t":"Motion to exceed page or word limits","d":"14 days from before the brief’s due date (7 days for a reply).\nGranted only for extraordinary and compelling reasons.","c":"GRAP 16(a)(7)(C)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"Granted only for extraordinary and compelling reasons."}},{"t":"Written motion to extend a brief deadline","d":"5 days from before the brief’s due date.\nOne oral clerk extension of up to 14 days is available first (GRAP 17(c)(1)).","c":"GRAP 17(c)(2)","due":{"n":5,"from":"the trigger date (before the brief’s due date)","note":"One oral clerk extension of up to 14 days is available first (GRAP 17(c)(1))."}},{"t":"Response to a motion","d":"7 days from service of the motion.\nNon-opposition statement due in the same 7 days. Reply due 5 days after the response.","c":"GRAP 6(a)(3)(A)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (service of the motion)","note":"Non-opposition statement due in the same 7 days. Reply due 5 days after the response."}},{"t":"Amicus brief","d":"7 days from filing of the supported party’s principal brief.","c":"GRAP 14(e)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the supported party’s principal brief)","note":""}},{"t":"Petition for rehearing (panel or en banc)","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\n45 days in a civil case where the Government of Guam or its officer or agency is a party.","c":"GRAP 30(a)(1), 29(c)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"45 days in a civil case where the Government of Guam or its officer or agency is a party."}},{"t":"Issuance of mandate","d":"7 days from expiration of rehearing time or denial of rehearing or stay motion.\nCalendar days.","c":"GRAP 27(c)(2)","due":{"n":7,"from":"the trigger date (expiration of rehearing time or denial of rehearing or stay motion)","note":"Calendar days."}},{"t":"Bill of costs","d":"14 days from entry of judgment.\nObjections within 10 days after service.","c":"GRAP 22(d)(1)","due":{"n":14,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment)","note":"Objections within 10 days after service."}},{"t":"Stay of mandate pending U.S. Supreme Court certiorari","d":"90 days from grant of the stay.\nContinues if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay.","c":"GRAP 27(c)(4)(B)(ii)","due":{"n":90,"from":"the trigger date (grant of the stay)","note":"Continues if the petition is filed and the Clerk notified within the stay."}},{"t":"Criminal appeal by defendant (for reference)","d":"10 days from entry of judgment or order (or government’s notice, if later).\nGovernment: 30 days (GRAP 4(b)(1)(B)).","c":"GRAP 4(b)(1)(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (entry of judgment or order)","note":"Government: 30 days (GRAP 4(b)(1)(B))."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://guamcourts.gov/"]]},"rulesName":"Guam Rules of Civil Procedure","rulesSrc":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"1","t":"Scope of rules","s":"The GRCP govern civil actions in the Superior Court of Guam. Most rules are taken from the 2003 Federal Rules of Civil Procedure (before the 2007 restyling and the 2009 time-computation changes), with Guam substitutions. Several rules (4, 6, 16, 47, 48, 55, 56) were updated in 2014, 2022 and 2023 to later federal text.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"2","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the civil action. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"3","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a complaint. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"4","t":"Summons","s":"Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). Any non-party 18 or older may serve. A waiver request must give the defendant at least 30 days to return it (60 days if outside Guam). A defendant who waives has 60 days from the request to answer (90 if outside Guam). A defendant in Guam or a U.S. jurisdiction who refuses to waive without good cause pays the later service costs and fees. Service on the Government of Guam goes through the Attorney General (Rule 4(i)). Service must be made within 90 days after filing; it was 180 days before the 2023 amendment. Rule 4(o) adds service by publication plus restricted-delivery mail on a party not found in Guam, under a statute or court order.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"4.1","t":"Service of other process","s":"Process other than a summons or subpoena is served by the marshal, a deputy marshal, or a specially appointed person, with proof under Rule 4(l). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"5","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Service on a represented party is made on the attorney: by hand, by leaving at the office or home, by mail (complete on mailing), or by consented electronic means (complete on transmission). Discovery papers are not filed unless ordered or used. Guam additions: Rule 5(f) allows fax filing on conditions. An off-island attorney with local counsel faxes to local counsel, who files the paper. Faxes after 5:00 p.m. on the due date are untimely. Originals are due within 14 days. Rule 5(g) lets the court serve attorneys by depositing papers in their Clerk’s-office box, complete on deposit. Rule 5(h) lets the court require a self-represented party without a Guam address to designate a Guam address or the clerk for service.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"6","t":"Time","s":"Amended by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). Every day now counts, including weekends and holidays. If the last day is a weekend, holiday, or a day the clerk’s office is inaccessible, the period runs to the next business day. Legal holidays include those in 1 GCA § 1000(a)–(b) and others declared by U.S. or Guam authorities. Before 2023, periods under 11 days excluded weekends and holidays. The court may extend time for good cause, or after expiry for excusable neglect, but never under Rules 50(b), (c)(2), 52(b), 59(b), (d), (e) and 60(b). Written motions and notice of hearing must be served at least 7 days before the hearing (it was 5 days). Opposing affidavits are due 1 day before. Add 3 days after service by mail, clerk-leaving or consented means (including e-service). In practice, motion briefing is governed by CVR 7.1.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"7","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Allowed pleadings: complaint, answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, and third-party complaint and answer. A reply to an answer is allowed only if ordered. Motions must be in writing with particular grounds. Demurrers and pleas are abolished. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"8","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"Short and plain statement of jurisdiction, claim and relief demanded. Denials must fairly meet the averments. Affirmative defenses must be pleaded, and the list still includes “discharge in bankruptcy.” Averments not denied are admitted. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"9","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Fraud or mistake must be pleaded with particularity; conditions of mind may be pleaded generally. Special damages must be specifically stated. The admiralty subdivision (h) is omitted. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"10","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption, numbered paragraphs, separate counts, and adoption by reference; exhibits are part of the pleading. Follows the 2003 federal rule. See also GR 5.1 (12-point minimum, title 3 inches from top) and CVR 10.1 (separate “jurisdiction” paragraph).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"11","t":"Signing; representations to court; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed, which certifies a proper purpose and support in law and fact. Safe harbor: a sanctions motion may not be filed until 21 days after service, and only if the paper is not withdrawn or corrected. Monetary sanctions are not allowed against a represented party for frivolous legal contentions. The rule does not apply to discovery. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"12","t":"Defenses and objections; motion for judgment on the pleadings","s":"Answer within 20 days after service (federal: 21). Cross-claim answers and counterclaim replies are also due within 20 days. The Government of Guam and its officers or agencies have 60 days after service on the Attorney General or agency counsel. After denial or postponement of a Rule 12 motion, the responsive pleading is due within 10 days, and within 10 days after a more definite statement is served. Motions to strike unanswerable pleadings are due within 20 days. Waiver rules for personal jurisdiction, venue and process defenses are the same as federal. Follows the 2003 federal rule (pre-2009 periods).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"13","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Compulsory counterclaims arise from the same transaction or occurrence; permissive counterclaims are allowed. Counterclaims against the Government of Guam are not enlarged beyond existing law. An omitted counterclaim may be added by leave. Cross-claims against co-parties are allowed. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"14","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defendant may implead without leave if the third-party complaint is filed within 10 days after serving the original answer (federal: 14), and otherwise by motion. A plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead likewise. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"15","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"One amendment as of course before a responsive pleading is served (or within 20 days if no response is permitted). After that, leave is freely given or written consent is needed. Respond to an amended pleading within the time left or 10 days, whichever is longer. Relation back follows the 2003 federal text; delivery to the Attorney General of Guam satisfies notice for Guam defendants. CVR 15.1 requires reproducing the entire amended pleading.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"16","t":"Pretrial conferences; scheduling; management","s":"Adopted from the 2007 federal rule by PRM 06-006-16 (July 29, 2014). The judge issues a scheduling order after the Rule 26(f) report or a conference, within the earlier of 120 days after any defendant is served or 90 days after any defendant appears. It must limit time to join parties, amend, complete discovery and file motions. The schedule is modified only for good cause. The final pretrial order is modified only to prevent manifest injustice. Sanctions apply for missed or unprepared conferences. See CVR 16.1–16.6 for local scheduling procedure.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"17","t":"Parties plaintiff and defendant; capacity","s":"Action is prosecuted in the name of the real party in interest; when a Guam statute so provides, it is brought in the name of the Government of Guam. Capacity and representation of minors and incompetents. Follows the 2003 federal rule and GRCP 17 (1990).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"18","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies","s":"A party may join as many claims as it has against an opposing party. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"19","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Required joinder where complete relief or protection of interests requires it; equity-and-good-conscience analysis when joinder is not feasible; the pleading must name absent persons and say why they are not joined. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"20","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Parties may join or be joined when claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence with a common question. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"21","t":"Misjoinder and non-joinder of parties","s":"Misjoinder is not a ground for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed on just terms. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"22","t":"Interpleader","s":"Claimants exposing the plaintiff to double or multiple liability may be required to interplead, even if their claims lack a common origin; available by cross-claim or counterclaim. Taken from GRCP 22 (1990); no statutory-interpleader counterpart.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"23","t":"Class actions","s":"Prerequisites, types, notice, and court approval of dismissal or compromise follow the 2003 federal rule. Guam’s 23(f): the Supreme Court may permit an appeal from an order granting or denying certification if application is made within 10 days after entry (federal: 14). The appeal does not stay proceedings unless ordered.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"23.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"Verified complaint, contemporaneous-ownership and demand allegations, adequacy of representation, and court approval of dismissal or settlement. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"23.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Members may sue or be sued as representatives of the association if they fairly and adequately protect its interests. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"24","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention on timely application. When the validity of a Guam Legislature act affecting the public interest is questioned in a case where the government is not a party, the court must notify the Attorney General. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"25","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"After death is suggested on the record, a motion to substitute must be made within 90 days or the action is dismissed as to the deceased party. Also covers incompetency, transfer of interest, and successors of public officers. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"26","t":"General provisions governing discovery; duty of disclosure","s":"Based on the 2003 federal rule. Initial disclosures are due within 14 days after the 26(f) conference, with Guam’s own list of 20 exempt proceedings. Expert disclosures are due at least 90 days before trial (rebuttal within 30 days). Pretrial disclosures are due 30 days before trial, with objections 14 days later. The 26(f) conference must be held at least 21 days before the scheduling conference, and the report is due within 14 days. Scope is the pre-2015 test: relevant to a claim or defense, or “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” with no proportionality clause. A committee note says “document” includes electronically stored information, but the 2006 federal ESI amendments were not adopted.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"27","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition to perpetuate testimony; notice must be served at least 20 days before the hearing. Also covers depositions pending appeal. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"28","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Within Guam or any U.S. jurisdiction, before an officer authorized by local law or appointed by the court. Abroad, by treaty, notice, commission or letter rogatory. Interested persons are disqualified. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"29","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Parties may stipulate to deposition and discovery procedures, except extensions that would interfere with court-set deadlines. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"30","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"Leave is required to exceed 10 depositions per side, to depose someone twice, or to depose early (unless the witness is about to leave Guam). Depositions are limited to one day of 7 hours (unless an interpreter is involved). The deponent has 30 days after notice to review and sign the transcript. Follows the 2003 federal rule. Under CVR 30.1, the original transcript goes to the noticing attorney.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"31","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Cross questions are due within 14 days after notice; redirect within 7 days; recross within 7 days. The same 10-deposition limit applies. Follows the 2003 federal rule (pre-2009 periods).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"32","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"A deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead, “outside of Guam” (the federal rule uses 100 miles), unable to attend, or not subpoenable, or in exceptional circumstances. It may not be used against a party who got less than 11 days’ notice and promptly moved for a protective order. Objections and waiver rules follow the 2003 federal text.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"33","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Up to 50 interrogatories including discrete subparts without leave (federal: 25). Answers or objections are due within 30 days, and objections must be specific. CVR 33.1 further limits each party to one set per opposing party and requires quoting each question in full in the answer.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"34","t":"Production of documents and things; entry upon land","s":"Requests must describe items with reasonable particularity. A written response is due within 30 days. Documents are produced as kept or organized by request category. Non-parties are reached by Rule 45 subpoena. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"35","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"The court may order an examination for good cause when a condition is in controversy; the report must be exchanged on request. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"36","t":"Requests for admission","s":"A matter is admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service. CVR 33.1 limits each party to one set per opposing party, capped at 50 requests including subparts. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"37","t":"Failure to make disclosure or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel, sanctions for violating orders, preclusion for failure to disclose, sanctions for failure to attend a deposition or respond, and sanctions for failing to help frame a discovery plan. Subdivisions (e) and (f) are omitted. CVR 37.1 requires a meet-and-confer and a joint written stipulation of the disputed issues before any discovery motion. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"38","t":"Jury trial of right","s":"The jury right under Guam law and the Organic Act is preserved. A written demand must be served no later than 10 days after service of the last pleading on the issue (federal: 14). Another party may demand other issues within 10 days. Failure to demand is a waiver.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"39","t":"Trial by jury or by the court","s":"Jury trial on demanded issues unless the parties consent otherwise or no jury right exists under the Organic Act or Guam law. The court may use an advisory jury. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"40","t":"Assignment of cases for trial","s":"Cases are calendared as provided by court rule, the Rules of the Superior Court, or Supreme Court administrative rule; statutory preferences apply. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"41","t":"Dismissal of actions","s":"The plaintiff may dismiss without order before an answer or summary-judgment motion is served, or by stipulation of all parties who have appeared. A second notice dismissal operates as an adjudication on the merits, counting a prior dismissal in any U.S. state, commonwealth or territory court. Involuntary dismissal and costs of a previously dismissed action follow federal practice. CVR 41.1: cases with no action for 6 months are called for status hearing and may be dismissed. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"42","t":"Consolidation; separate trials","s":"Consolidation of actions with common questions; separate trials preserving the jury right under the Organic Act and Guam law. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"43","t":"Taking of testimony","s":"Testimony is taken in open court unless Guam law, these rules, or Title 6 GCA (Evidence) provide otherwise. Remote testimony is allowed for good cause in compelling circumstances. Motions may be heard on affidavits. The court may appoint interpreters. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"44","t":"Proof of official record","s":"Authentication of domestic and foreign official records by official publication or attested copy with certificate. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"44.1","t":"Determination of foreign law","s":"A party raising foreign law must give written notice. The court may consider any relevant source, including materials not admissible under Title 6 GCA (Evidence). The ruling is treated as a ruling on a question of law. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"45","t":"Subpoena","s":"Drawn from the 2003 federal rule and the 2005 Nevada rules. The clerk issues subpoenas signed in blank, or attorneys may sign them. Any non-party 18 or older may serve, tendering one day’s fees and mileage (none for Government of Guam subpoenas). Subpoenas may be served anywhere in Guam. Written objections to production are due within 14 days after service, or before the compliance date if sooner. Parties must get prior notice of document subpoenas. Subpoenas for actions pending outside Guam may be issued by the clerk.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"46","t":"Exceptions unnecessary","s":"Formal exceptions are unnecessary; making known the action desired and the grounds is enough. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"47","t":"Selecting jurors","s":"Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24 (Oct. 19, 2023). The court or the parties may examine jurors. Peremptory challenges are allowed in the number set by 7 GCA § 22120 (not collected). The court may excuse a juror for good cause during trial or deliberation. The former alternate-juror subdivision was removed.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"48","t":"Number of jurors; verdict; polling","s":"Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24. A jury begins with at least 6 and no more than 12 members. Unless the parties stipulate otherwise, the verdict must be unanimous from at least 6 jurors. Polling is required on a party’s request.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"49","t":"Special verdicts and interrogatories","s":"Special verdicts and general verdicts with interrogatories; rules for inconsistent answers. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"50","t":"Judgment as a matter of law; alternative new-trial motion","s":"A JMOL motion may be made before submission to the jury. A renewed motion is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28), and cannot be extended. A new-trial motion by a party against whom JMOL is entered is also due within 10 days. Conditional new-trial rulings follow the federal rule. Since Oct. 19, 2023, GRCP 6(a) counts these as calendar days.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"51","t":"Instructions to jury; objections; preserving a claim of error","s":"Requests for instructions, the court’s duty to disclose proposed instructions, on-the-record objections before instructions are given, and plain-error review. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 51.1 governs submission of proposed instructions, voir dire questions and verdict forms.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"52","t":"Findings by the court; judgment on partial findings","s":"In non-jury trials, the court finds facts specially and states conclusions of law; findings are set aside only if clearly erroneous. A motion to amend findings is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment (federal: 28). Judgment on partial findings is allowed. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 52.1 governs proposed findings.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"53","t":"Masters","s":"Appointment by consent, for exceptional conditions, or for matters a judge cannot timely address, with a disqualification affidavit under 28 U.S.C. § 455. Objections to a master’s order, report or recommendations are due within 20 days after service. Findings are reviewed de novo unless the parties stipulate otherwise. Follows the 2003 federal rule, including the magistrate-judge reference.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"54","t":"Judgments; costs","s":"Rule 54(b) certification of partial final judgment. A default judgment may not exceed the demand. Costs go to the prevailing party, but against the Government of Guam only as permitted by law. The clerk taxes costs on 1 day’s notice, with review on motion within 5 days. A fee motion is due no later than 14 days after entry of judgment (also CVR 54.3). Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 54.1 governs the bill of costs.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"55","t":"Default; default judgment","s":"Restyled to the 2021 federal text by PRM 06-006-24. The clerk enters default and may enter judgment for a sum certain against a non-appearing, competent defendant. Otherwise apply to the court. A party who has appeared must get written notice at least 7 days before the hearing (it was 3 days). Default is set aside for good cause, and a default judgment under Rule 60(b). A default judgment against the Government of Guam requires satisfactory evidence.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"56","t":"Summary judgment","s":"Rewritten to the 2010 and later federal text by PRM 06-006-18-01 (July 18, 2022). Standard: no genuine dispute of material fact. Unless the court orders otherwise, a motion may be filed until 30 days after the close of all discovery. Materials must be cited. Covers unavailable facts (56(d)), judgment independent of the motion (56(f)), and bad-faith declarations (56(h)). CVR 56.1 requires a statement of issues and a “Statement of Undisputed Material Facts” with record cites, and a response to it.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"57","t":"Declaratory judgments","s":"Procedure follows these rules; jury rights are preserved. Another adequate remedy does not bar declaratory relief, and a speedy hearing may be ordered. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"58","t":"Entry of judgment","s":"Every judgment must be on a separate document, except orders on Rule 50(b), 52(b), 54 (fees), 59 and 60 motions. The clerk enters simple judgments; the court approves others. Entry occurs on docketing plus the earlier of the separate document or 150 days. A timely fee motion may be given Rule 59 tolling effect for appeal under GRAP 4. Follows the post-2002 federal text. CVR 58.1 requires following Official Forms 31 or 32.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"59","t":"New trials; amendment of judgments","s":"New-trial grounds are those historically recognized in Guam’s courts. A new-trial motion is due no later than 10 days after entry of judgment, and so is a motion to alter or amend (federal: 28 for both). Opposing affidavits are due within 10 days, extendable up to 20. The court may order a new trial on its own within 10 days. None of these times may be extended (Rule 6(b)(2)). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"60","t":"Relief from judgment or order","s":"Clerical mistakes may be corrected at any time; after an appeal is docketed, only with leave of the appellate court. Rule 60(b) grounds (mistake, new evidence, fraud, void, satisfied, any other reason) must be raised within a reasonable time, and not more than 1 year for reasons (1)–(3). Independent actions are preserved. To toll the appeal time under GRAP 4(a)(4), file within 10 days after judgment. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"61","t":"Harmless error","s":"Errors not affecting substantial rights are disregarded. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"62","t":"Stay of proceedings to enforce a judgment","s":"Execution is automatically stayed for 10 days after entry (federal: 30), except for injunction and receivership judgments. Stays are available pending post-judgment motions. A supersedeas bond stays the judgment when the court approves it. The Government of Guam need not post a bond. Appellate power to stay is preserved. Follows the 2003 federal rule (pre-2018 text).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"63","t":"Inability of a judge to proceed","s":"A successor judge may continue a trial or hearing after certifying familiarity with the record. In bench trials, material disputed witnesses must be recalled on request. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"64","t":"Seizure of person or property","s":"Pre-judgment seizure remedies are available as provided by the law of Guam. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"65","t":"Injunctions","s":"A preliminary injunction requires notice. A TRO without notice requires an affidavit or verified complaint showing immediate irreparable injury and an attorney certification of notice efforts. It expires within the time fixed, not over 10 days, extendable once for a like period. The adverse party may move to dissolve on 2 days’ notice. Security is required, except from the Government of Guam and from parties in domestic-relations or abuse cases. Every order must state reasons and be specific. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"65.1","t":"Security: proceedings against sureties","s":"Sureties submit to the court’s jurisdiction and appoint the clerk as agent for service; liability is enforced by motion. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"66","t":"Receivers appointed by the courts","s":"Administration of receiverships follows past Guam practice or Supreme Court rules. A receivership action may be dismissed only by court order. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"67","t":"Deposit in court","s":"Money or things in dispute may be deposited with the court on notice and by leave. The depositing party serves the order on the clerk. Money must be placed in an interest-bearing account or instrument approved by the court (a Guam addition). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"68","t":"Offer of judgment","s":"A defending party may serve an offer more than 10 days before trial. The offeree has 10 days to accept in writing. If the final judgment is not more favorable than the offer, the offeree pays the costs incurred after the offer. A post-liability offer must be made at least 10 days before the damages hearing. Follows the 2003 federal rule (federal now: 14 days).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"69","t":"Execution and garnishment","s":"Guam’s own rule (GRCP 69 (1990), amended July 19, 2010). A writ of execution is optional; enforcement may also follow the GCA. The clerk may disallow needless costs where multiple writs issue. A claimed wage exemption must be set for hearing not less than 5 days later. If the plaintiff does not join issue on a garnishee’s answer within 5 days after notice, the garnishee gets judgment. Garnishee examinations require leave of court. A plaintiff garnishing third-party property must file and mail a notice of exemptions within 10 days after the writ issues. Revenue-officer judgments are satisfied under Guam law.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"70","t":"Judgment for specific acts; vesting title","s":"If a party fails to perform as directed, the court may direct another to perform at that party’s cost, hold the party in contempt, or vest title to property in Guam by judgment. Former 70(a) on repossession deficiencies was deleted, and the subject is left to the Guam UCC (Title 13 GCA). Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"71","t":"Process in behalf of and against persons not parties","s":"Orders in favor of or against non-parties are enforceable as if they were parties. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"71A","t":"Condemnation of property","s":"Eminent-domain procedure follows the 2003 federal rule. The notice tells defendants to serve an answer within 20 days. Defendants who live in Guam at a known address are served personally under Rule 4(c)–(d). Otherwise, on an attorney’s certificate, service is by publication in a Guam newspaper of general circulation once a week for at least 3 successive weeks, with mailing to any known address before the last publication.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"72","t":"Vacant","s":"The federal magistrate-judge rule has no Superior Court counterpart.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"73","t":"Vacant","s":"See note to Rule 72.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"74","t":"Vacant","s":"See note to Rule 72.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"75","t":"Vacant","s":"See note to Rule 72.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"76","t":"Vacant","s":"See note to Rule 72.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"77","t":"Courts and clerks","s":"The courts are always open for filing. Trials are in open court; other acts may be done in chambers anywhere in Guam. The clerk’s office is closed weekends and holidays. Guam additions let the clerk grant certain orders without the judge: consent satisfactions, Rule 58 and default judgments, judgment-debtor examination orders and OSCs under 7 GCA §§ 23201, 23207, fee waivers, and stipulated or confessed judgments. The clerk mails notice of entry of each order or judgment. Lack of notice does not extend the appeal time except as GRAP allows.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"78","t":"Motions","s":"Each judge sets regular motion days and may decide motions on written submissions without oral hearing. Follows the 2003 federal rule. CVR 7.1 controls briefing: opposition 28 days after the motion is filed, reply 42 days after filing, 20-page and 10-page limits, and a request for oral argument within 7 days after the last paper.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"79","t":"Books and records kept by the clerk","s":"The clerk keeps the civil docket and other records in the form the Presiding Judge prescribes with Judicial Council approval. Entries of orders and judgments show their date of entry. Follows the 2003 federal rule and GRCP 79 (1990).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"80","t":"Stenographer; transcript as evidence","s":"A certified transcript of testimony may be used to prove that testimony where relevant. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"81","t":"Vacant","s":"No applicability-in-general rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"82","t":"Jurisdiction unaffected","s":"The rules do not extend or limit the Superior Court’s jurisdiction.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"83","t":"Vacant","s":"Omitted because local practice rules are the Rules of the Superior Court (the CVR and GR).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"84","t":"Forms","s":"The appendix forms suffice under the rules. Follows the 2003 federal rule.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"85","t":"Title","s":"The rules may be cited as the Guam Rules of Civil Procedure (GRCP).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"86","t":"Effective date","s":"The revised rules took effect June 1, 2007. They apply to pending cases unless that would be infeasible or unjust (Preamble).","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"87","t":"Vacant","s":"Now Rule 85.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"88","t":"Vacant","s":"Now Rule 86.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"89","t":"Vacant","s":"Formerly listed Code of Civil Procedure sections superseded by the GRCP; the list was dropped.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"90","t":"Fee exemption for Government of Guam","s":"No filing fees or costs (except reporter transcript fees) are charged to a Government of Guam agency funded by annual General Fund appropriation. The agency must file a Certificate of Waiver (Form 35) signed under penalty of perjury.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"91","t":"Omitted","s":"Fees are now set by the Judicial Council, not the GRCP.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"92","t":"Omitted","s":"Small-claims rules are now in the Miscellaneous Rules.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"},{"n":"93","t":"Omitted","s":"Records-retention rules are now in the Miscellaneous Rules.","u":"https://guamcourts.gov/sites/default/files/GRCP%2020240401_Control.pdf"}]});
